      "definitions": {
        "title": {
          "text": "Why composability wins"
        },
        "narrative": {
          "width": "100%",
          "text": "The history of frontend development is a history of abstractions. We went from raw HTML to CSS frameworks, from component libraries to design systems. Each jump was supposed to solve the handoff problem — the gap between what designers imagine and what engineers ship.\n\nBut something changed. The tools got better, but the gap stayed the same. Designers were still pushing Figma files over the wall. Engineers were still translating them into JSX.\n\nJSON-driven layout is the next attempt to close that gap. Not by adding another tool — but by making the interface itself the artifact. When a page is a plain JSON document, anyone can read it, version it, fork it, and ship it. The renderer becomes an interpreter. The designer becomes an author.\n\nThe key insight: layout decisions are data. Column widths, spacing rhythms, typographic scale, entrance animations — all of these are values, not code. When you treat them as data, you gain composability for free."
        },
        "meta-heading": {
          "text": "In this article"
        },
        "meta-list": {
          "text": "→ Why composability wins\n→ The abstraction problem\n→ JSON as interface\n→ What comes next"
        },
        "meta-link": {
          "preset": "type-h5-meta",
          "text": "∞ 12 min read"
        }
      }
    },
